The final match of the [[1974 FIFA World Cup]] was held in [[Munich]], [[West Germany]] (now [[Germany]]) on 7 July. It was the 10th [[FIFA World Cup]] competition, held to determine the world champion among national men's [[association football|football]] sides. The match was contested by the [[Netherlands national football team|Netherlands]] and [[Germany national football team|West Germany]], with West Germany winning 2–1. The Netherlands opened the scoring via a [[Johan Neeskens]] penalty in the second minute, only for [[Paul Breitner]] to equalise with another penalty in the 25th minute before [[Gerd Müller]] scored the winning goal in the 43rd minute, claiming West Germany's second FIFA World Cup title.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/world_cup_2006/4850426.stm |title=World Cup history - West Germany 1974 |work=BBC Sport |publisher=British Broadcasting Corporation |date=4 May 2006 |access-date=27 July 2012 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20060706173225/http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/world_cup_2006/4850426.stm |archive-date=6 July 2006 |url-status=live }}</ref>

Five German players ([[Sepp Maier]], [[Franz Beckenbauer]], [[Wolfgang Overath]], [[Jürgen Grabowski]] and [[Horst-Dieter Höttges]]) became the first in history to have won gold, silver and bronze medals at the FIFA World Cup.

